# ============================================================
#  Mini-plots (2026-10-25): short storylines of four to six cards, at the places whose visits most often came up
#  empty (grim report --gaps: the infirmary, the lecture tower, the home town). Each is a @thread from the story
#  template (docs/grimoire-script.md §9): its start card begins it, and every other card waits on that without saying
#  so. Plot-safe: none of them touches the main mystery.
#    amaranth_rounds  — the night Sister Amaranth missed her round (infirmary, lakeshore)
#    red_ink          — someone marks your essays in red ink (lecture tower)
#    late_bloomer     — the sweet shop's daughter and her sparks (home, across two breaks)
# ============================================================

# ---------------- The missed round ----------------

@thread amaranth_rounds
from: story
with: begun=flag(rounds_begun)
class: story
repeat: 1
tags: story
clues: lead_missed_round
members:
  start infirmary_missed_round

@event infirmary_missed_round
title: The Lamp That Went Out
place: infirmary
time: evening
tone: tense
when: met(amaranth) and term and turn >= 6 and not flag(rounds_begun)
repeat: 3
weight: 14
art: lantern
scene: A dim infirmary ward at evening, a fever charm over the far bed glowing a dull red, a healer's desk lamp unlit.
text: The fever charm over the far bed has burned down to a red glow. It should have been turned an hour ago. Sister Amaranth does every round herself, and has never once missed one. Her lamp is dark.
> left: Turn the charm and find her
  health -tiny
  flag rounds_begun
  find lead_missed_round
  queue infirmary_sister_fevered now
  say: You turn the charm the way you've seen her do it, a quarter-turn and a tap. Then you go looking for the lamp.
> right: It isn't your ward
  mood -tiny
  say: You tell yourself she's only late. On your way out, the patient in the far bed is still awake, watching the dark.

@event infirmary_sister_fevered
title: The Healer's Own Fever
thread: amaranth_rounds
trigger: queued
tone: tense
art: potion
scene: A small office behind a hospital ward, a grey-faced healer upright in her chair with a compress on her neck and a list of patients in her lap.
text: You find her in the little office behind the ward, upright in her chair, a cold compress on her own neck and the patient list in her lap. Her face is grey. "Lake Fever," she says. "From bed six. It's nothing. Go to bed."
> left: Fetch the Headmistress
  alignment +tiny
  rel amaranth -small
  flag rounds_reported
  queue infirmary_covering_rounds in 1
  say: The headmistress comes in her dressing gown, and two healers from the village by morning. Sister Amaranth doesn't speak to you for a week. Then she thanks you, as an instruction: "Next time, run faster."
> right: Do the rounds she dictates
  health -small
  rel amaranth +small
  flag rounds_secret
  queue infirmary_covering_rounds in 1
  say: "Bed two, the blue bottle, half a spoon. Bed four, turn the charm. Bed six…" She stops. "Bed six will lie to you. Don't believe her." You do the rounds until midnight.

@event infirmary_covering_rounds
title: Eight Beds, One of You
thread: amaranth_rounds
trigger: queued
slot: evening
tone: tense
when: not flag(rounds_covered)
art: cauldron
scene: A row of infirmary beds at evening, a student in rolled-up sleeves measuring a remedy while a healer gives orders from a chair.
text: {flag(rounds_secret): Sister Amaranth still can't stand, and is still giving orders from her chair.|The village healers are run off their feet, and Sister Amaranth, propped up in her chair, is giving them orders too.} Eight beds, eight remedies, one of you. A second-year with a burned hand wants to know if it will scar.
> left: Do every bed properly
  study -tiny
  challenge brew apprentice+:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      rel amaranth +small
      flag rounds_covered
      say: Every bed, every spoon, every charm turned on the hour. The burned hand won't scar. "Adequate," says the chair, which from her is a medal.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      flag rounds_covered
      say: You mix up beds three and five. Nobody notices but her. "Again," she says, and you do the whole row again.
  queue lakeshore_fever_runaway now
> right: Do the urgent ones, then revise
  rel amaranth -tiny
  flag rounds_covered
  queue lakeshore_fever_runaway now
  say: You see to the burns and the fevers and leave the rest for the morning. From the chair comes a small, disappointed silence.

@event lakeshore_fever_runaway
title: Bed Six Goes Missing
thread: amaranth_rounds
trigger: queued
tone: tense
when: flag(rounds_covered) and not flag(runaway_back)
art: wave
scene: A first-year in a hospital nightshirt standing knee-deep in a dark lake at dusk, feeling along the bottom with both hands.
text: Bed six is empty, and the window by it is open. You find her where Sister Amaranth said you would: knee-deep in the lake at dusk, shivering, feeling along the bottom. "I lost my mother's ring," the first-year says through chattering teeth. "Swimming. Where we weren't supposed to."
> left: Wade in and search with her
  challenge feat apprentice wild:
    win:
      health -small
      vigor +small
      flag ring_found
      say: Your fingers close on it among the reeds: a thin silver band, slippery with weed. She stops shivering long enough to cry. You both walk back dripping.
    lose:
      health -medium
      say: Nothing but cold and stones. When her lips go blue you carry her back up the hill, and Sister Amaranth's face says everything her fever won't let her.
  flag runaway_back
  queue infirmary_tonic_key in 2
> right: Talk her back to bed
  challenge appeal apprentice:
    win:
      charm +small
      flag ring_found
      say: You promise to look for it in daylight, and she lets you walk her back. Next morning you rake the shallows with a borrowed net, and find the ring on the third pass.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -small
      say: She won't come. You wait on the shore with her until she's too cold to argue, then walk her back with your cloak round her.
  flag runaway_back
  queue infirmary_tonic_key in 2

@event infirmary_tonic_key
title: The Key to the Cupboard
thread: amaranth_rounds
trigger: queued
slot: evening
class: milestone
tone: warm
when: flag(rounds_covered) and flag(runaway_back)
art: key
scene: A healer on her evening round holding out a small brass key beside a glass-fronted cupboard of tonics, not looking at the student she offers it to.
text: Sister Amaranth is back on her feet, doing her evening round as if nothing happened. At the end of it she stops at the tonic cupboard, unhooks a small brass key and holds it out without looking at you. {flag(ring_found): Bed six wears a silver ring on a string round her neck.|Bed six is asleep, for once.}
> left: Take the key
  rel amaranth +medium
  flag amaranth_key
  flag rounds_finished
  say: "Keep doing that," she says. It's the most anyone has ever said to you in this room. The key is warm from her pocket.
> right: "I'd rather not be trusted with it."
  rel amaranth +small
  mood +small
  flag rounds_finished
  say: She hangs it back on its hook. "Good answer," she says. "Wrong, but good." She gives you a tonic anyway, the good one.

# ---------------- Red ink ----------------

@thread red_ink
from: story
with: begun=flag(red_ink_begun)
class: story
repeat: 1
tags: study
clues: lead_red_ink
members:
  start lecture_tower_red_ink

@event lecture_tower_red_ink
title: Two Sets of Marks
place: lecture_tower
time: morning afternoon
tone: light
when: turn >= 4 and not flag(red_ink_begun)
repeat: 3
weight: 14
art: scroll
scene: A student on a spiral stair reading an essay covered in neat pencil and, beneath it, furious corrections in red ink.
text: Your returned essay has two sets of marks. The marker's, in polite pencil: "Good." And another, in red ink and unsigned, savaging every paragraph and fixing three of them. The last line reads: "Again. Properly."
> left: Rewrite it properly
  mood -tiny
  lore +medium
  study +tiny
  flag red_ink_begun
  find lead_red_ink
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_reply in 1
  say: You rewrite it twice. On the third morning you leave it on the second-floor stair, where you found the first. By evening it's gone.
> right: Bin it
  mood +tiny
  say: Good is good. You drop the red pages in the scorched-room bin, and feel a little foolish about how much you want to fish them out.

@event lecture_tower_red_ink_reply
title: A Question in the Margin
thread: red_ink
trigger: queued
tone: light
when: not flag(red_ink_answered) and not flag(red_ink_glimpse)
art: paper
scene: An essay left on a stone stair, its pages bleeding red ink, a single question written small at the bottom of the last page.
text: The essay is back on the stair, bleeding red. Most of it is about your argument. At the bottom, smaller: "Why do you want to be good at this?" It's the first question the ink has asked.
> left: Answer it honestly in the margin
  lore +tiny
  mood -tiny
  flag red_ink_answered
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_tremble in 1
  say: You write three lines you'd never say aloud, and leave the essay on the stair. It's gone by noon. You spend the afternoon wishing you'd written two.
> right: Wait on the stair to see who takes it
  challenge deceive master- likely:
    win:
      guile +small
      say: You hide one flight up. A grey sleeve, scorched at the cuff, gathers the pages without a sound. You know exactly one person whose cuffs are scorched.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      say: You fall asleep on the stair. When you wake the essay is gone, and there's a note on your chest: "Hm."
  flag red_ink_glimpse
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_tremble in 1

@event lecture_tower_red_ink_tremble
title: The Reading List
thread: red_ink
trigger: queued
tone: warm
when: (flag(red_ink_answered) or flag(red_ink_glimpse)) and not flag(red_ink_seen_hand)
art: book
scene: Four pages of red-ink notes spread on a desk, the downstrokes of the handwriting wavering, a long reading list pinned on top.
text: This time the red ink answers at length: four pages on your essay, and a reading list. The downstrokes waver, as if written in a moving carriage. {seen(thorne_shaking_hands) > 0: You've seen hands shake like that, gripping a lectern.|On the lower floors, Professor Thorne writes on the board very slowly these days, and very large.}
> left: Read the whole list
  health -small
  lore +medium
  study +tiny
  flag red_ink_seen_hand
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_office in 1
  say: You read every book on the list, mostly at night. One has a library card stamped forty years ago, with a single name on it: A. Thorne.
> right: Take the notes, skip the list
  lore +small
  flag red_ink_seen_hand
  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_office in 1
  say: You take the notes and leave the reading list in your satchel. It looks at you every time you open it.

@event lecture_tower_red_ink_office
title: Improper Marking
thread: red_ink
trigger: queued
slot: afternoon
class: milestone
tone: warm
actor: thorne
when: flag(red_ink_seen_hand)
art: scroll
scene: An old professor at a cluttered office desk looking down at a stack of red-inked essays, a capped red pen in his trembling hand.
text: Office hours. You put the stack of red-inked essays on Thorne's desk. He looks at them a long time. "Improper," he says. "Marking essays that aren't mine. I write very few letters, you understand. I like to know who I'm writing them for."
> left: "Then write one for me."
  challenge appeal master- +1 if rel(thorne) >= 15:
    win:
      rel thorne +medium
      renown +small
      flag thorne_letter
      say: "Hm." From him, that's a yes. The letter, when it goes, is four lines long. The Conclave, you hear later, read it twice.
    lose:
      rel thorne +small
      say: "Not yet," he says. "Again. Properly." It takes you a day to understand that it was a promise.
  flag red_ink_finished
> right: "Thank you. Keep marking them."
  rel thorne +medium
  lore +small
  flag red_ink_finished
  say: He nods once. The red ink keeps coming to the end of the year. It never once says "good". Once, it says "better".

# ---------------- The late bloomer ----------------

@thread late_bloomer
from: story
with: begun=flag(bloomer_begun)
class: story
repeat: 1
tags: magic
clues: lead_late_bloomer
members:
  start home_market_late_bloomer

@event home_market_late_bloomer
title: Blue Candles
place: home_market
tone: warm
when: not flag(bloomer_begun)
repeat: 4
weight: 16
art: candle
scene: A small sweet shop window full of jars, every candle in it flaring blue, a young woman staring at her own hands beside a dropped tray.
text: The sweet shop is the one thing in town that hasn't changed. Then the owner's daughter, a year or two older than you, drops a tray, swears, and every candle in the window flares blue. Her father looks at you, then at your castle robes. "You're the one who'd know," he says quietly.
> left: Show her how to breathe it down
  mana -small
  arcana +small
  flag bloomer_begun
  find lead_late_bloomer
  say: You show her the first thing anyone at the castle learns: breathe in for four, hold the heat, let it go for four. The candles settle. She looks at her hands as if they belonged to someone else.
> right: "It happens. It'll settle."
  mood -tiny
  say: Her father nods, glad to be told so. Behind him she relights the candles one by one, with a match, very carefully.

@event home_fields_bloomer_lessons
title: Lessons on the Wall
thread: late_bloomer
place: home_fields
tone: light
when: not flag(bloomer_steady)
weight: 16
art: flame
scene: A young woman and a student in castle robes on a low field wall at sunset, the young woman holding a candle stub at arm's length, its flame far too tall.
text: She's waiting on the low wall at the end of the long rows, a candle stub in each pocket. "Teach me something," she says. "Properly. Before you go back."
> left: Teach her the candle drill
  challenge spell apprentice+:
    win:
      arcana +small
      mood +small
      flag bloomer_steady
      queue home_cottage_bloomer_father in 1
      say: By sunset she can hold a flame the size of a fingernail for ten breaths. She counts every one of them out loud.
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: The candle goes off like a firework. You both lie in the grass laughing until the farmer shouts. "Next time," she says.
> right: Spend the afternoon at home
  health +small
  say: You promise her next time. She shrugs, the way people do when they've stopped expecting things.

@event home_cottage_bloomer_father
title: Sweets on the Step
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
tone: warm
when: flag(bloomer_steady) and not flag(bloomer_father_seen)
defer: not has(plan_home)
art: door
scene: A worried shopkeeper at a cottage door holding a small paper bag of sweets, not stepping inside.
text: Her father is at the door with a paper bag of the sweets you liked when you were small. He doesn't come in. "They take them young, at the castle, don't they," he says. "She's past it. So what is it for, all that fire, in a sweet shop?"
> left: Tell him the truth
  mood -small
  alignment +tiny
  flag bloomer_father_seen
  queue home_market_bloomer_charm in 1
  say: "I don't know what it's for," you tell him. "I don't think anyone does, at her age." He thanks you for not lying, and leaves the sweets on the step.
> right: Promise to ask at the castle
  mood +small
  flag bloomer_father_seen
  flag bloomer_promise
  queue home_market_bloomer_charm in 1
  say: His face does something complicated. "Would you," he says, not quite a question. You hear yourself promise. The sweets taste exactly as they used to.

@event home_market_bloomer_charm
title: A Lid on the Pot
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
slot: afternoon
tone: tense
when: flag(bloomer_father_seen) and not flag(bloomer_charm_done)
defer: not has(plan_home)
art: gem
scene: A market charm seller holding up a grey glass bead on a cord to a young woman counting coins from her apron.
text: In the market you find her at the charm seller's table. He's holding up a grey glass bead on a cord. "Smothers it," he's saying. "Like a lid on a pot. No more flares, no more fuss." She's counting out her savings.
> left: Help her pay, since she wants it
  gold -small
  mood -small
  flag bloomer_damped
  flag bloomer_charm_done
  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1
  say: You make up what she's short, because it's her choice. She puts the bead on. The shop's candles burn an ordinary yellow from then on. She looks lighter, and smaller.
> right: Talk her out of it
  challenge appeal apprentice+:
    win:
      charm +small
      queue home_market_bloomer_ice now
      say: You tell her about the first-years who set their sleeves alight and are now the best in their year. She puts her coins back in her apron, slowly.
    lose:
      mood -small
      flag bloomer_damped
      queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1
      say: She buys it anyway. "Easy for you," she says, not unkindly. "You get to go back."
  flag bloomer_charm_done

@event home_market_bloomer_ice
title: Ice in the Square
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
class: incident
tone: tense
when: flag(bloomer_charm_done) and not flag(bloomer_damped) and not flag(bloomer_ice_done)
art: snow
scene: A market-square horse trough frozen solid around a carter's arm, a panicking young woman beside it and frost spreading across the cobbles.
text: Not ten minutes later, a shout from the square. The horse trough has frozen solid, with a carter's arm in it to the elbow, and she's standing beside it white with panic. Frost is creeping across the cobbles from her feet.
> left: Break the ice by hand
  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:
    win:
      health -small
      vigor +small
      say: You hammer at the ice with a cobble until it gives with a crack like a door. The carter pulls his arm free, swearing, mostly at the trough.
    lose:
      health -medium
      say: You get him free in the end, but the ice takes the skin off your knuckles doing it. You'll feel that trough for days.
  flag bloomer_ice_done
  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1
> right: Talk her down so it melts
  challenge appeal adept-:
    win:
      charm +small
      mood +small
      say: "Four in. Hold. Four out." Her breath slows, and the ice goes grey, then soft, then water. The carter stares at his arm, and then at her.
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -small
      say: It takes an hour, three kettles from the inn and a carter who will tell this story for years.
  flag bloomer_ice_done
  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1

@event home_cottage_bloomer_letter
title: A Flame That Stays
thread: late_bloomer
trigger: queued
slot: evening
defer: not has(plan_home)
class: milestone
tone: warm
when: flag(bloomer_charm_done) and (flag(bloomer_ice_done) or flag(bloomer_damped)) and not flag(bloomer_finished)
art: candle
scene: A young woman at a cottage table at evening, a small steady candle flame burning on the tip of her finger.
text: {flag(bloomer_damped): She comes by one evening, the grey bead on its cord. "I keep it in my pocket now," she says. "Not on. Just in case." She lights a candle with her finger, small and steady, to show you.|She comes by one evening with a candle she lit at noon and has kept burning, small and steady, all day. "Look," she says. "It stays."}
> left: Write to the castle for her
  gold -tiny
  mood +medium
  flag bloomer_letter
  flag bloomer_finished
  say: {flag(bloomer_promise): You keep your promise to her father and write to the castle that night|You write to the castle that night}: what she can do, what she learned with no teacher but you, and that some people come late to things. You pay the postage; she walks it to the post office herself.
> right: Give her your first-year book
  study -tiny
  mood +medium
  flag bloomer_finished
  say: {flag(bloomer_promise): You never did ask at the castle. Instead you leave her your battered first-year book, margins and all.|You leave her your battered first-year book, margins and all.} A week later a letter comes: "Your notes are wrong. I've fixed them."
